WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.767
-Our next guest
is a talented actor you know

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:04.434
from shows like "The Punisher"
and "The Walking Dead."

00:00:04.434 --> 00:00:07.501
He stars in "The Premise," which
debuts its first two episodes

00:00:07.501 --> 00:00:10.234
on FX, on Hulu this Thursday,

00:00:10.234 --> 00:00:12.033
and in
"The Many Saints of Newark,"

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:14.300
which is in theaters
and HBO Max October 1st.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.767
He also produced and stars
in "Small Engine Repair,"

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:19.801
which is in theaters now.
Let's take a look.

00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:21.667
-Oh, yes!
Oh, yes!

00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:23.667
Who's my sexy man?

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:27.033
Do you like me
so, so [bleep] much?

00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:29.734
God, did I miss this man.

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:32.167
You bought a new TV, huh?
-I did.

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:36.267
-Yeah, mine's, uh --
mine's bigger, but that's good.

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:37.968
-Please welcome to the show
Jon Bernthal.

00:00:37.968 --> 00:00:40.133
How are you, Jon?
-What's going on, man?

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:41.400
Thank you for having me.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.234
-Now, wait, is this --
This is a big deal for us.

00:00:43.234 --> 00:00:44.634
I think this is the first time
this has happened.

00:00:44.634 --> 00:00:47.100
Is that the same dog
we just saw from the clip?

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.601
-Oh, no! No, no.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:48.601 --> 00:00:50.868
-That is not.
No, that -- this is --

00:00:50.868 --> 00:00:52.634
This is Bam Bam.
That was Boss.

00:00:52.634 --> 00:00:55.534
Sadly, we -- that --
that was Boss's final movie.

00:00:55.534 --> 00:00:57.701
Sadly, we --
we lost him after that one.

00:00:57.701 --> 00:00:59.400
But, look,
we started talking about him,

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.300
he immediately left the --
left the couch.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.767
But Bam Bam was in the movie.
He plays Boss in the flashbacks.

00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:06.501
So, you know, we were --
we had all kinds.

00:01:06.501 --> 00:01:08.100
Everybody's dogs
were in that movie.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.300
Everybody who made the movie,
their kids were in the movie.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.334
It was -- It was definitely
a labor of love.

00:01:12.334 --> 00:01:15.000
-I like that Bam Bam left, like,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.000
they had established
with our team, like,

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.033
"Do not ask about the dog."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:21.801
Like, that --
I almost wish your dog

00:01:21.801 --> 00:01:24.400
had taken off a microphone
as it left.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.834
-He le-- He left in anger.

00:01:25.834 --> 00:01:27.300
He was like,
"Do not talk about me.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.067
I did not sign up for this."

00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:32.300
-So, this is really cool,
because this is a play

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.234
you did I guess
about a decade ago.

00:01:35.234 --> 00:01:37.067
It takes place in New Hampshire,
where I'm from.

00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:38.200
I know you're not
from New Hampshire,

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.200
but you have spent
some time there.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.267
-I love New Hampshire.
I -- I -- you know,

00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:44.501
New Hampshire is --
some of my greatest memories

00:01:44.501 --> 00:01:46.868
are -- are from New Hampshire.
I love the state.

00:01:46.868 --> 00:01:48.734
I love everything
the state stands for.

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:52.968
I worked there in -- at a summer
camp when I was younger.

00:01:52.968 --> 00:01:56.934
And then, just this year,
I dropped my two little boys off

00:01:56.934 --> 00:01:59.934
at that same summer camp,
which, I have to tell you --

00:01:59.934 --> 00:02:02.133
I mean, they're 8 and 9.
They're pretty young.

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:03.767
And so to say goodbye to them
for a month

00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:05.200
was definitely very difficult.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.968
But, you know, I --
They loved it.

00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:09.667
They loved being there.

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:12.334
You know,
my boys are very different.

00:02:12.334 --> 00:02:16.801
My 9-year-old, Henry, he --
it was rough for him.

00:02:16.801 --> 00:02:19.133
You know, he -- he had --
You could just see sort of

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:20.868
the tears bubbling up
in his eyes.

00:02:20.868 --> 00:02:22.968
And, you know, I was --
was being a dad, you know,

00:02:22.968 --> 00:02:24.334
and I gave him a big hug,
and he said,

00:02:24.334 --> 00:02:26.067
"Dad, I think I'm gonna cry."

00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:27.501
And I said, "Man,
just go ahead, let it out.

00:02:27.501 --> 00:02:30.133
I'm right here."
And we kind of touched noses.

00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:32.234
And he said, "No, Dad,
I'm gonna be strong for you."

00:02:32.234 --> 00:02:34.200
And he turned around
and walked away.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.701
And as that happened,
my other son, Bill,

00:02:36.701 --> 00:02:39.200
just smacked me in the ass
and he said,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.234
"See you around, fat man,"
and just walked off

00:02:41.234 --> 00:02:42.467
in the other direction.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:44.467
So, you know, they're --
they're very different.

00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:46.667
But I think New Hampshire
was just as good for them

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:49.501
as it was for me.
-That's fantastic.

00:02:49.501 --> 00:02:51.133
This is --
You know, obviously,

00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:54.200
a movie set
is a very controlled atmosphere.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.534
But this
is a really intense story,

00:02:57.534 --> 00:03:01.067
which I imagine, on stage,
there were less sort of

00:03:01.067 --> 00:03:04.234
safety guardrails
when you were going through

00:03:04.234 --> 00:03:06.267
performing a piece like this.

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:08.601
-Look, the --
the play started in --

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:10.801
in sort of as humble a beginning
as possible.

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:13.067
It was in this teeny little
40-seat theater.

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:15.734
It was a 10:30-at-night play.

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:17.934
You know, what was really
wonderful about it, man,

00:03:17.934 --> 00:03:21.534
is that a lot of my friends
came who are cops, firemen,

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:22.968
pro fighters,
guys who don't really

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:24.734
come to the theater very often.

00:03:24.734 --> 00:03:26.501
And they were mixing with this,
you know,

00:03:26.501 --> 00:03:28.767
super progressive
L.A. theater scene.

00:03:28.767 --> 00:03:32.901
And what we found was sort of
this explosive, kind of --

00:03:32.901 --> 00:03:36.567
really just -- just beautiful --
Everyone just really dug it.

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:38.100
Every--
It -- It was.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.767
It was dangerous.
It was exciting.

00:03:39.767 --> 00:03:42.100
It made you think.
And I think the trick was,

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:44.400
"How do you capture that
in a film?"

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.834
And I really do --
I really do think that,

00:03:47.834 --> 00:03:50.534
you know, what's very special
about this is,

00:03:50.534 --> 00:03:52.634
you know, this film, it will --

00:03:52.634 --> 00:03:53.901
you know,
it's gonna make you laugh.

00:03:53.901 --> 00:03:55.300
You know, there --
there are times where it will

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.467
definitely make you cry.

00:03:56.467 --> 00:03:58.167
It might offend
the heck out of you.

00:03:58.167 --> 00:04:00.100
But it -- it --
more than anything else,

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:01.667
I think it will
really make you think.

00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:05.300
And I think we captured
what was special about the play

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.467
and got it into the film.

00:04:07.467 --> 00:04:10.067
-You have an incredibly
unique -- it strikes me,

00:04:10.067 --> 00:04:13.567
at least -- journey,
insofar as where you got

00:04:13.567 --> 00:04:15.834
your acting training.
You did it in the States,

00:04:15.834 --> 00:04:17.667
but you also found
your way to Russia.

00:04:17.667 --> 00:04:20.133
Who told you it would be a good
idea to -- to go train there

00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:22.968
and, in the end, did it end up
being a good call?

00:04:22.968 --> 00:04:27.434
-Look, I'm very grateful for --
for -- for my time in Russia.

00:04:27.434 --> 00:04:29.400
I went there, I played a --
I played a little bit of

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.767
pro baseball over there,

00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:33.000
and that's where I really
studied acting.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.167
And I think I would not
be doing what I'm doing now

00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:37.434
if it wasn't for that.

00:04:37.434 --> 00:04:40.501
I'm enormously grateful,
you know, to be doing this.

00:04:40.501 --> 00:04:44.367
And it was a teacher.
I think you can't undervalue

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:47.667
or underestimate
the power that a teacher has.

00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:50.067
My teacher -- my first acting
teacher was Alma Becker.

00:04:50.067 --> 00:04:52.968
She's the woman who ended up
marrying my wife and I.

00:04:52.968 --> 00:04:55.467
She sent me to Russia.
She -- She knew --

00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:58.234
I think probably to get away
from my maniac friends

00:04:58.234 --> 00:05:00.534
would probably be a good thing
to get as far away as possible.

00:05:00.534 --> 00:05:02.501
Maybe that had something
to do with it.

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:05.033
But I think the theater training
in Russia, you know,

00:05:05.033 --> 00:05:07.834
it's extremely disciplined.

00:05:07.834 --> 00:05:10.267
And, you know,
I'm a -- I'm a boxer.

00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:12.100
I played college sports.
I played football.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.701
I played baseball.
There is nothing that came close

00:05:14.701 --> 00:05:16.934
to the discipline and the rigor
of the Russian theater.

00:05:16.934 --> 00:05:19.467
And, you know, I'm --
I'm very grateful that I kind of

00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:22.634
learned this thing over there
rather than here.

00:05:22.634 --> 00:05:25.601
-You also -- I've read that
when you first started acting,

00:05:25.601 --> 00:05:30.033
your goal was just to be
an extra in "The Sopranos."

00:05:30.033 --> 00:05:33.534
And there's nothing I'm looking
forward to more than

00:05:33.534 --> 00:05:36.300
"The Many Saints of Newark,"
which is the "Sopranos" film

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.000
that's coming out,
that David Chase has obviously

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.467
been kind enough
to give to the world.

00:05:40.467 --> 00:05:44.234
How exciting was it to --
to get to enter that universe?

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:45.968
-Look, you know, a blessing.

00:05:45.968 --> 00:05:49.334
You know,
super humbling and exciting.

00:05:49.334 --> 00:05:51.133
Yeah.
I mean, all I wanted was

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:54.400
just an audition to --
to try to get onto that set.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:55.901
That's when I was sort of,

00:05:55.901 --> 00:05:57.367
you know,
starting out as an actor.

00:05:57.367 --> 00:06:03.534
And, look, I can't tell you how
honored I am to be a part of it.

00:06:03.534 --> 00:06:06.868
I think what Michael Gandolfini,
you know, does in this movie

00:06:06.868 --> 00:06:08.534
and to sort of be a part
of his journey

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:12.067
and stepping into, you know,
his father's shoes,

00:06:12.067 --> 00:06:13.501
I just feel really honored

00:06:13.501 --> 00:06:15.968
that I get to, you know,
walk alongside him.

00:06:15.968 --> 00:06:18.000
And I think he approached it
with courage,

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.534
and he approached it with,
you know, real dedication.

00:06:21.534 --> 00:06:24.667
And, you know, my job there
was to sort of stand by him,

00:06:24.667 --> 00:06:27.267
and I believe in this young man,
and I think he's going to --

00:06:27.267 --> 00:06:30.167
I think people are gonna
really enjoy what he does.

00:06:30.167 --> 00:06:32.667
-Also, I've heard, you know,
there are certain things

00:06:32.667 --> 00:06:36.067
where even I can't
get an early copy of it.

00:06:36.067 --> 00:06:37.167
-Oh, no.
-I'm trying.

00:06:37.167 --> 00:06:38.567
Can't get it.
But I've heard

00:06:38.567 --> 00:06:40.601
it's really [bleep] good.
Is it good?

00:06:40.601 --> 00:06:42.467
-Man, look, to --
to be honest with you,

00:06:42.467 --> 00:06:44.534
I haven't seen it myself.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:44.534 --> 00:06:46.734
-I feel better now.
I feel better now.

00:06:46.734 --> 00:06:49.534
-What I will say is that,
you know, look, it's different.

00:06:49.534 --> 00:06:51.634
I think people that are gonna
go in and expecting

00:06:51.634 --> 00:06:54.200
"The Sopranos," it's not that.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:57.167
I think that it was very smart
that they set it in the past

00:06:57.167 --> 00:06:58.567
and that it's a genuine prequel.

00:06:58.567 --> 00:07:01.467
What you will see as being
a real sort of "Sopranos" fan

00:07:01.467 --> 00:07:05.634
is that, I think that, you know,
David Chase has this sort of

00:07:05.634 --> 00:07:08.767
encyclopedic kind of
Shakespearean knowledge

00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:10.968
of these characters.
And you will see things --

00:07:10.968 --> 00:07:13.501
you will see that when he was
writing this show, you know,

00:07:13.501 --> 00:07:15.968
during its heyday,
he clearly understood

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:18.767
the full history of who
these characters were.

00:07:18.767 --> 00:07:20.234
And I think
that's just kind of --

00:07:20.234 --> 00:07:22.300
You know, the real super fans,
I think it's just gonna be

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.067
so much fun.

00:07:24.067 --> 00:07:25.601
And I think the performances,
you know,

00:07:25.601 --> 00:07:27.934
Vera did and Corey
and Alessandro --

00:07:27.934 --> 00:07:29.901
I mean, the performances
are gonna be great.

00:07:29.901 --> 00:07:31.734
I know that, 'cause I saw
what they were doing.

00:07:31.734 --> 00:07:33.667
But, yeah, they haven't
showed it to me, either, yet.

00:07:33.667 --> 00:07:35.000
-All right, well,
thank you very much

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.167
for making me feel better
about myself.

00:07:37.167 --> 00:07:38.467
Congratulations on everything,

00:07:38.467 --> 00:07:40.033
and thanks so much
for making the time for us.

00:07:40.033 --> 00:07:41.934
-I appreciate it, man.
Thank you for having me.

00:07:41.934 --> 00:07:43.801
-"Small Engine Repair"
is in theaters now.

